course: dictionary
lesson: 10021
title: Tend: habits and looking after
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tend has two main meanings. The first is about habits. «Live shows often go wrong a little; audiences tend to remember the fun more than the mistakes.» Here tend to means usually. To check, swap in usually: audiences usually remember the fun, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The second meaning is looking after someone or something. Nurses tend the sick, and you can tend a garden by watering and weeding it. The clue is in the job: a nurse helps people who are ill, so tend means to care for them.
explain: In science and maths, tends to describes a pattern. «Studies suggest that people who often give thanks tend to feel happier.» This does not say every person feels happier. It says the pattern is common. Spelling matters too: tend, tends, tended and tending.
fact: Tend means to usually do something, or to often be a certain way.
fact: Tend also means to look after someone or something, as in tend the garden or tend a wound.
fact: «Tend to» shows a habit: «I tend to get tired in the afternoon» means I usually get tired then.
fact: Tends is used with he, she, it or one thing; tended is the past form; tending is the -ing form.
exercise: «I tend to get tired in the afternoon.» What does tend to mean here? | Forget everything | Run very fast | *Usually do something | Look after someone
exercise: «Hiding mistakes tends to make them bigger.» What does tends to show? | Mistakes always disappear | Hiding is a game | *Hiding mistakes usually makes them bigger | Hiding mistakes looks after them
exercise: Nurses tend the sick. What does tend mean here? | *To look after | To hide | To finish | To usually do
test: Which meaning of tend is in «tend the garden»? | Ignore | Dig up | *Look after | Usually do something
test: «Taller children tend to have longer feet.» What does this sentence show? | *A common pattern, not true for everyone | Children stop growing | A rule that every tall child has long feet | Feet are never long
exam: Yesterday the nurse ___ the sick children all night. | tends | tend | tending | *tended
